This can be fixed with the rust 1.91 feature floor_char_boundary or other methods if you want to stick with older rustc.

```
result.content_text.truncate(result.content_text.floor_char_boundary(options.max_extracted_len));
```

I had Claude Code do some memory profiling of my content ingestion pipeline and came across a couple of potential improvements, including this one:

---

## Problem

`Readability::parse` allocates more memory at peak than it needs to. On the [Rust (programming language) Wikipedia page](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rust_(programming_language)) (1.05 MB of HTML, 9.39 MB of heap after `Readability::new`), `parse()` adds another 10.15 MB at peak. With the change below it adds 4.24 MB, and the extracted article is identical.

Each attempt in `grab_article` clones `self.doc`, and dom_query's `Tree::clone` gives the node array no spare capacity. The first node the attempt creates (a `<p>` from `div_into_p`, or the article `<div>`) pushes onto a full `Vec`, which reallocates to twice the size and copies every node (160 bytes each) while both buffers are live. So each attempt costs a clone, plus a second, twice-as-large copy, plus a moment where both are alive.

Repro: `cargo new dq-repro-smoothie`, then:

```toml
[dependencies]
dom_smoothie = { git = "https://github.com/niklak/dom_smoothie", branch = "main" }
```

<details><summary><code>src/main.rs</code> (counting allocator + synthetic 20,000-paragraph article)</summary>

```rust
use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering::Relaxed};

// Counts a realloc as old and new buffers live at once, which is what a moving realloc does.
struct Counting;
static LIVE: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
static PEAK: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
static BIG_ALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
static BIG_REALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
const BIG: usize = 1 << 20;
// dom_query's node array is a Vec of 160-byte, 8-aligned TreeNodes; text buffers are 1-aligned.
fn is_node_array(l: &Layout, n: usize) -> bool { n >= BIG && l.align() == 8 && n % 160 == 0 }

fn add(n: usize) {
    let now = LIVE.fetch_add(n, Relaxed) + n;
    PEAK.fetch_max(now, Relaxed);
}

unsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Counting {
    unsafe fn alloc(&self, l: Layout) -> *mut u8 {
        if is_node_array(&l, l.size()) { BIG_ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Relaxed); }
        add(l.size());
        System.alloc(l)
    }
    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, p: *mut u8, l: Layout) {
        LIVE.fetch_sub(l.size(), Relaxed);
        System.dealloc(p, l)
    }
    unsafe fn realloc(&self, p: *mut u8, l: Layout, n: usize) -> *mut u8 {
        if is_node_array(&l, n) { BIG_REALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Relaxed); }
        add(n);
        let r = System.realloc(p, l, n);
        LIVE.fetch_sub(l.size(), Relaxed);
        r
    }
}

#[global_allocator]
static A: Counting = Counting;

fn mb(b: usize) -> String { format!("{:.2} MB", b as f64 / 1e6) }

fn main() {
    // Pass an HTML file, or get a synthetic article: 20,000 <div>s of inline text,
    // each of which `div_into_p` turns into a <p>.
    let html = match std::env::args().nth(1) {
        Some(path) => std::fs::read_to_string(path).unwrap(),
        None => {
            let mut s = String::from("<html><head><title>t</title></head><body><article>");
            for i in 0..20_000 {
                s += &format!("<div>Paragraph {i}, with some words, and commas, to score.</div>");
            }
            s + "</article></body></html>"
        }
    };

    let mut r = dom_smoothie::Readability::new(html, None, None).unwrap();
    let base = LIVE.load(Relaxed);
    PEAK.store(base, Relaxed);
    BIG_ALLOCS.store(0, Relaxed);
    BIG_REALLOCS.store(0, Relaxed);

    let article = r.parse().unwrap();

    println!("document after parse:  {}", mb(base));
    println!("parse() peak:          +{}", mb(PEAK.load(Relaxed) - base));
    println!("parse() live after:    +{}", mb(LIVE.load(Relaxed) - base));
    println!("node-array allocs:     {} (document clones)", BIG_ALLOCS.load(Relaxed));
    println!("node-array reallocs:   {} (growth by doubling)", BIG_REALLOCS.load(Relaxed));
    println!("text_content:          {} chars", article.text_content.chars().count());
}
```

