`cargo run --release` on `main` @ 51c7f93:

```
document after parse:  11.80 MB
parse() peak:          +19.21 MB
parse() live after:    +5.71 MB
node-array allocs:     1 (document clones)
node-array reallocs:   1 (growth by doubling)
text_content:          1088890 chars
```

With the fix below:

```
document after parse:  11.80 MB
parse() peak:          +7.58 MB
parse() live after:    +3.40 MB
node-array allocs:     1 (document clones)
node-array reallocs:   0 (growth by doubling)
text_content:          1088890 chars
```

Across 17 real article pages from our crawler (5 KB to 1.6 MB of HTML), summed `parse()` peak above the parsed documents went from +32.14 MB to +16.50 MB. `text_content` and `content` were byte-identical on all 17.

## Cause

`src/grab.rs`:

```rust
pub(crate) fn grab_article(&self, metadata: &Metadata) -> Option<Document> {
    let mut flags: FlagSet<GrabFlags> = FlagSet::full();
    let mut best_attempt: Option<(Document, usize)> = None;
    loop {
        let doc = self.doc.clone();
        let article_node = self.attempt_grab_article(&doc, &flags, metadata);
```

The attempt then creates nodes on the clone: `<p>`s in `wrap_phrasing_content` (via `div_into_p`, 20,000 of them on the synthetic page), the article `<div>` in `handle_candidates`, and `<img>`s in `prep_article`. dom_query's `Tree::clone` is `Vec::clone`, which allocates exactly `len`, so the first new node doubles the array. `self.doc` usually has spare capacity left over from parsing, and it sits unused while the attempt runs on the full clone.

## Suggested fix

Run the attempt on `self.doc` and keep the clone as the pristine copy. The attempt's new nodes land in the parse slack, and the exact-size clone is only ever cloned again, never appended to. Same nodes, same ids, so the output does not change (`parse_with_policy` already runs its single attempt on `self.doc` in place). No dom_query change is needed; `grab_article` now takes `&mut self`:

```rust
pub(crate) fn grab_article(&mut self, metadata: &Metadata) -> Option<Document> {
    let mut flags: FlagSet<GrabFlags> = FlagSet::full();
    let mut best_attempt: Option<(Document, usize)> = None;
    loop {
        // Run the attempt on `self.doc` and keep the clone as the pristine copy for the
        // next attempt. `Tree::clone` allocates exactly `len` nodes, so the first node
        // an attempt creates (a `<p>` from `div_into_p`, the article `<div>`) would
        // double a clone's node array. `self.doc` usually still has spare capacity
        // left over from parsing, so the attempt fits in place. This relies on parse
        // leaving spare capacity: if `Tree` ever shrinks to fit after parsing, the gain
        // disappears.
        let pristine = self.doc.clone();
        let doc = std::mem::replace(&mut self.doc, pristine);
        let article_node = self.attempt_grab_article(&doc, &flags, metadata);
        // ... unchanged
```

`parse_impl` already holds `&mut self`. The only other change is `let mut ra` in the `grab_article` unit test. If every attempt fails, `self.doc` is left as the untouched clone, the same state as today. All of `cargo test` passes with the change. Later attempts still clone from an exact-size copy, so a `Tree::reserve` in dom_query would be the more general fix. A PR for the swap can follow if this approach looks right.

